142	<h3><span class="num">Change 36 &middot; committing.md, &ldquo;Pushing&rdquo;</span>The branch rule holds in a cloud container</h3>
143	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in</span>
144	<p class="where-in">committing.md, &ldquo;Pushing&rdquo;, line 41, the bullet after the bullet on pushing a worker&rsquo;s own branch</p>
145	<ul><li><span class="add">From a session in a cloud container, do</span> <span class="cut">Do</span> not try to delete a branch on GitHub<span class="cut">: the automatic permission check refuses it, and the curator deletes it.</span><span class="add">, or retry a refusal with another tool (the <code>cloud-container</code> skill, &ldquo;Rules for every agent&rdquo;).</span> Removing a worktree does not delete its branch, which stays. Ask nobody in your report to delete a branch.</li></ul>
146	</div></div>
147	<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule&rsquo;s meaning: a worker still does not try to delete a branch on GitHub. What moved is the fact behind it. The old sentence said that the automatic permission check refuses such a deletion, as if every session met that refusal. It happens only in a session in a cloud container. A session on the curator&rsquo;s own machine has no such check, and the old sentence would tell it something false. At the curator&rsquo;s comments of 06:13 and 06:15 UTC on 7 November, the fact now stands once, in the <code>cloud-container</code> skill, &ldquo;Rules for every agent&rdquo;, and this bullet points there. The bullet also says that a session does not retry a refusal with another tool. The first wording only stated that a cloud session cannot delete a branch; a cold read found that it gave no action, so the fixed text says what to do. The two sentences after it did not change.</p></div>
148	</section>
